# Schema class definition specs lookup/extraction process

**Your role:** An expert in schema.org and JSON-LD.

**Your goal:** Extract the `[CLASS_NAME]` schema definition spec and specs for all its **own** proeprties. Do not include specs of properties of other classes where `[CLASS_NAME]` is as a value, but do include the spec of the requested class itself. 
Example:

```
CLASS_NAME: Occupation

// (OK) included - the class definition spec
    {
      "@id": "schema:Occupation",
      "@type": "rdfs:Class",
      "rdfs:comment": "A profession, may involve prolonged training and/or a formal qualification.",
      "rdfs:label": "Occupation",
      "rdfs:subClassOf": {
        "@id": "schema:Intangible"
      },
      "schema:source": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/1698"
      }
    },

// (OK) included - it is a property "occupationLocation" of Occupation class
    {
      "@id": "schema:occupationLocation",
      "@type": "rdf:Property",
      "rdfs:comment": " The region/country for which this occupational description is appropriate. Note that educational requirements and qualifications can vary between jurisdictions.",
      "rdfs:label": "occupationLocation",
      "schema:domainIncludes": {
        "@id": "schema:Occupation"  // "schema:domainIncludes" has "@id": "schema:Occupation"
      },
      "schema:rangeIncludes": {
        "@id": "schema:AdministrativeArea"
      },
      "schema:source": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/1698"
      }
    },

// (NOT OK) not included. It is a property "hasOccupation" of Person class
    {
      "@id": "schema:hasOccupation",
      "@type": "rdf:Property",
      "rdfs:comment": "The Person's occupation. For past professions, use Role for expressing dates.",
      "rdfs:label": "hasOccupation",
      "schema:domainIncludes": {
        "@id": "schema:Person"
      },
      "schema:rangeIncludes": {
        "@id": "schema:Occupation"
      },
      "schema:source": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/1698"
      }
    },
```


**Before starting**: note the schema class name user is asking about. Use it to substitute `[CLASS_NAME]` in the following instructions.

**Process:**

1. Use text search / grep in `packages/schemas/src/raw-definitions/schema.org.current.jsonld` file contents for accurances of "schema.[CLASS_NAME]" string. You don't need to look anywhere else.

2. For each and every JSONLD object containing the accurances of "schema.[CLASS_NAME]", check if the "schema.[CLASS_NAME]" is referenced in `schema:domainIncludes`. Do not include other objects in the results output.

3. Output the full list of the filtered results as JSONLD code snippets. DO NOT SKIP OR TRUNCATE ANYTHING

4. Write results to a new file in packages/schemas/src/raw-definitions/by-class/[CLASS_NAME].json

**Restrictions:**
- do not search any other files, rather than .jsonld. DO NOT USE any .ts files in this particular task.
- be strict, precise and exhaustive
- NEVER SUGGEST